<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth was a federated state created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. It was one of the largest states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, notable for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elected monarchy</a>, powerful noble parliament and relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. Weakened by internal paralysis and foreign intervention, it was partitioned out of existence by its neighbours by 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth.jpg" alt="Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olek Remesz (wiki-pl: Orem, commons: Orem) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chor%C4%85giew_kr%C3%B3lewska_kr%C3%B3la_Zygmunta_III_Wazy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> had been in a personal union since the Krewo Agreement of 1385 and the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of Poland to Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of Lithuania, who was crowned Władysław II Jagiełło. Lithuania's vulnerable position persuaded its nobles to seek a closer bond with Poland.</p><p>Lesser Lithuanian nobles wanted to share the privileges of the Polish szlachta, but they refused Polish demands to incorporate the Grand Duchy as a mere province. Mikołaj Radziwiłł the Red and his cousin Mikołaj Radziwiłł the Black opposed the union. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> established the Commonwealth as a single entity on 1 July 1569, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> kept its own name, laws and territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> died childless in 1572, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, and monarchs were thereafter elected for life. On 11 May 1573 Henry de Valois was proclaimed king in the first royal election outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in which about 40,000 nobles voted. He signed the Pacta conventa and approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrician-articles">Henrician Articles</a>, but in June 1574 he left to claim the French crown, and the throne was declared vacant.</p><p>At its peak in the early 17th century the Commonwealth, spanned about 1,000,000 square kilometres and some 12 million people in 1618. Its stability then waned after a series of blows, as persecution of religious and ethnic minorities led several groups to rebel.</p><p>In the 18th century foreign powers interfered in royal elections and bribed nobles; Frederick Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was elected in June 1697 with financing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> and Habsburg Austria and crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a>. In 1764 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> was elected with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>; in 1773 the king and parliament formed the Commission of National Education, the first Ministry of Education in European history. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a> was a late reform effort, but the Commonwealth was partitioned among Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the late 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The political system, called the Golden Liberty, had a bicameral parliament, the General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, whose lower house was elected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a>, about 10 percent of the population, and an elected king bound by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrician-articles">Henrician Articles</a> and the pacta conventa. Power slipped increasingly from the central government to the nobility. The Commonwealth showed unusual ethnic diversity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> by European standards, guaranteed by the Warsaw Confederation Act of 1573, though the practical degree of freedom varied.</p><p>Polish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> were the official languages, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> the state religion. The economy rested chiefly on agriculture and trade, with workshops such as paper mills, tanneries, ironworks and glassworks concentrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>, separated the powers of government and extended political rights beyond the nobility to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1772 and 1795 Prussia, the Austrian Empire and the Russian Empire divided the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b> in three Partitions of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cossack Hetmanate, officially the Zaporozhian Host, was a stratocratic state built by Registered Cossacks in Dnieper Ukraine out of the Khmelnytsky Uprising of 1648 against the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Raids into <b>Poland-Lithuania</b> and Muscovy seized men, women and children who were sold through the slave market of Caffa, under direct Ottoman rule, to buyers across the Ottoman Empire and the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A privilege from Sigismund II Augustus in 1560 gave it the rights of the Kraków University, making it one of the leading universities of the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its dynastic union with Poland, begun at Krewo in 1385, produced the Jagiellonian dynasty, which at times ruled Poland, Lithuania, Bohemia and Hungary, and ultimately the <b>Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal Prussia's autonomy ended in 1569 with the Union of Lublin, which created the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
